# ============================================================
# Modelfile for devstral-small-2:24b-instruct-2512-q8_0
# M5 Pro, 48GB unified memory — coding agent (Claude Code / Zed)
# Build: ollama create devstral-coder -f Modelfile.devstral
# ============================================================

# Devstral Small 2 — 24B dense params, 384K context, Q8_0.
# Q8_0 preserves near-full precision weight quality — worthwhile
# for agentic tasks where reasoning quality compounds across a
# long tool-use loop. Fits in ~26GB of unified memory, leaving
# ~22GB headroom on your 48GB M5 Pro.
FROM devstral-small-2:24b-instruct-2512-q8_0

# ── Sampling parameters ─────────────────────────────────────

# Mistral's official recommendation for Devstral 2 in agentic
# use is 0.15 — lower than general chat models. Raised slightly
# to 0.2 to avoid degenerate repetition on long file outputs,
# while staying firmly in the deterministic range. Use 0.15
# if you find the model making inconsistent tool call decisions.
PARAMETER temperature 0.2

# top_p: nucleus sampling cutoff. 0.95 is Mistral's default
# for this model family and works well at low temperature.
PARAMETER top_p 0.95

# top_k: candidate token pool at each step. 20 is Mistral's
# recommended value for Devstral — intentionally tight to
# reinforce deterministic code generation.
PARAMETER top_k 20

# Mild repetition penalty. Code legitimately repeats patterns
# (variable names, loop constructs, boilerplate) so keep this
# low. 1.05 discourages only true pathological loops without
# interfering with normal code structure.
PARAMETER repeat_penalty 1.05

# Disable presence penalty entirely — penalising token presence
# hurts identifier reuse in code, which is often intentional.
PARAMETER presence_penalty 0.0

# ── Context window ──────────────────────────────────────────

# Devstral Small 2 supports 384K context natively. With 26GB
# for the Q8 model and ~22GB headroom, 65536 tokens is the
# safe default — covers large multi-file agent sessions
# comfortably. KV cache at 65536 tokens costs roughly 8-10GB
# for a dense 24B model, keeping total usage well under 40GB.
#
# You can raise to 131072 for full-repo context if needed —
# watch Activity Monitor for memory pressure. Do not attempt
# 256K+ without setting OLLAMA_KV_CACHE_TYPE=q8_0 first to
# compress the KV cache.
PARAMETER num_ctx 65536

# Batch size for prompt ingestion (prefill). 512 is the sweet
# spot for Apple Silicon — large enough to parallelise long
# prompt evaluation during initial context load, conservative
# enough to avoid memory spikes on large codebases.
PARAMETER num_batch 512

# ── Hardware ────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Force all 40 layers onto the GPU via Metal. On Apple Silicon,
# CPU and GPU share unified memory so there is no transfer
# overhead — everything runs on-chip at full M5 Pro bandwidth.
PARAMETER num_gpu 999

# M5 Pro: 8 performance cores + 4 efficiency cores (12 total).
# 10 threads targets all performance cores plus 2 efficiency
# cores, leaving headroom for Zed, Claude Code, and the OS.
# Lower to 8 if you notice thermal throttling under sustained load.
PARAMETER num_thread 10

# No cap on generated tokens — let the model complete full
# file edits and multi-step tool call chains uninterrupted.
PARAMETER num_predict -1

# ── System prompt ───────────────────────────────────────────

# Note: when using Claude Code via `ollama launch claude`,
# Claude Code injects its own system prompt at runtime and
# this SYSTEM block is effectively overridden. It still
# applies for direct Zed agent usage and `ollama run` sessions.
#
# Derived from Mistral's recommended OpenHands-style prompt,
# trimmed for general coding agent use without scaffold
# assumptions baked in.
SYSTEM """You are Devstral, an expert software engineering assistant built by Mistral AI. You help users write, edit, debug, and reason about code.

<ROLE>
Your primary role is to assist users by reading files, editing code, running shell commands, and solving technical problems effectively. Be thorough and methodical — prioritise correctness over speed.
- If the user asks a question (e.g. "why is X happening"), answer it directly. Do not silently attempt a fix.
- If a request is ambiguous, ask for clarification before proceeding.
- When editing existing code, preserve the author's conventions, style, and structure.
</ROLE>

<EFFICIENCY>
Each action in an agentic loop has a cost. Where possible:
- Combine multiple bash commands into one invocation.
- Use grep, find, and git commands with appropriate filters before reading whole files.
- Make focused, minimal edits — avoid rewriting code that does not need to change.
- Never guess at file contents or structure; use your tools to verify first.
</EFFICIENCY>

<CODE_QUALITY>
- Prefer explicit, readable solutions over clever ones.
- Always include necessary imports and handle errors appropriately.
- Briefly explain trade-offs when multiple approaches exist.
- For shell commands, prefer macOS/Unix idioms.
- You can read images but cannot generate them.
</CODE_QUALITY>"""
